+### [Coarse-to-Fine Sphere Tracing](4615-coarse-to-fine-sphere-tracing.md)
+Accelerates rendering by initiating the trace at a lower image resolution and progressively refining it. It exploits spatial coherence, allowing neighboring rays to skip redundant computations. Choose this for scenes with high spatial coherence where standard sphere tracing performs many redundant samples at full resolution.
 
 ### [Sphere Tracing with SDFs](6223-sphere-tracing-with-sdfs.md)
-Sphere Tracing is an image-order rendering algorithm for implicit surfaces defined by Signed Distance Functions (SDFs). It solves the problem of finding the intersection between a camera ray and complex, continuous geometry without requiring explicit polygonal meshes or bounding volume hierarchies. It sets itself apart from its sibling [Ray-marching on 3D Gaussians](1036-ray-marching-on-3d-gaussians.md) by using exact geometric distance bounds rather than probabilistic density accumulation, and from [Terrain Raymarching](1357-terrain-raymarching.md) by supporting arbitrary 3D topology rather than height-field constraints. It builds on [Distance-field ray marching](21-distance-field-ray-marching.md).
+The standard implementation for rendering analytic SDFs. It constructs the image directly from equations, allowing for complex, continuous geometry without explicit meshes. Choose this for basic implicit surface rendering where no acceleration structures or multi-resolution strategies are required.
+
+### [Bidirectional Sphere Tracing](7433-bidirectional-sphere-tracing.md)
+A robust surface extraction technique that mitigates convergence failures when rays miss surface edges or intersect non-orthogonally. It uses forward and backward iterations to narrow the possible range of surface positions, then refines the zero-level set. Choose this for scenes with thin features or complex topology where standard sphere tracing may fail to converge.
+
+### [Relaxed Sphere Tracing](9463-relaxed-sphere-tracing.md)
+An acceleration technique that increases the step size beyond the conservative SDF value using a relaxation factor $\omega$. It reverts to the standard safe step if the new sample indicates the ray has jumped over the surface. Choose this when you need faster convergence on smooth surfaces and can tolerate occasional conservative fallbacks.
+
+### Triangle Inequality Enhancement for Sphere Tracing
+An optimization to eliminate unnecessary distance computations for collections of objects by using the triangle inequality to bound the distance to an object based on previous evaluations and ray traversal [S1]. It is particularly effective for large assortments of objects [S1]. However, if all primitives are nearly equidistant from the eye, the triangle inequality does not significantly reduce unnecessary distance evaluations until sphere tracing has traversed much of each ray [S1]. For 9 spheres/plane, it reduced execution time from 2:53 to 1:19 (46% relative time) [S1]. For the zeno logo, it reduced execution time from 26:29 to 19:23 (73% relative time) [S1]. When combined with convexity, it enhances ordinary sphere tracing by 60% [S1].
 
 ### Chunked Sphere Tracing
-Processes rays in batches to manage memory or compute limits.
-- [S1, unofficial] Get total number of rays N from ray_bundle.origins.shape[0].
-- [S1, unofficial] Initialize empty list chunk_colors.
-- [S1, unofficial] Loop over chunks: for chunk_start in range(0, N, self._chunk_size).
-- [S1, unofficial] Slice ray_bundle for current chunk: cur_ray_bundle = ray_bundle[chunk_start : chunk_start + self._chunk_size].
-- [S1, unofficial] Call parent forward method: cur_color = super().forward(sampler, implicit_fn, cur_ray_bundle).
-- [S1, unofficial] Append color to list: chunk_colors.append(cur_color).
-- [S1, unofficial] Concatenate all chunk colors along dimension 0: color = torch.cat(chunk_colors, dim=0).
-- [S1, unofficial] Return concatenated color.
-- [S1, unofficial] chunk_size: maximum number of rays processed in a single forward pass.
+A memory management technique for processing large numbers of rays by dividing them into chunks. The algorithm gets the total number of rays $N$ from `ray_bundle.origins.shape[0]` [S2, unofficial]. It initializes an empty list `chunk_colors` [S2, unofficial]. It loops over chunks: `for chunk_start in range(0, N, self._chunk_size)` [S2, unofficial]. It slices the ray bundle for the current chunk: `cur_ray_bundle = ray_bundle[chunk_start : chunk_start + self._chunk_size]` [S2, unofficial]. It calls the parent forward method: `cur_color = super().forward(sampler, implicit_fn, cur_ray_bundle)` [S2, unofficial]. It appends the color to the list: `chunk_colors.append(cur_color)` [S2, unofficial]. Finally, it concatenates all chunk colors along dimension 0: `color = torch.cat(chunk_colors, dim=0)` [S2, unofficial]. The key parameter is `chunk_size`, which defines the maximum number of rays processed in a single forward pass [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Origin: The core sphere tracing algorithm was introduced by Turner Whitted in 1980 [S1, unofficial]. It is widely used in real-time graphics for rendering complex implicit surfaces, such as those generated by Fractal Brownian Motion (FBM) or neural networks, where explicit mesh generation is prohibitive [6223-sphere-tracing-with-sdfs.md].
+Sphere tracing was introduced by Turner Whitted in the context of ray tracing, but the specific geometric method for antialiased ray tracing of implicit surfaces using signed distance bounds was formalized by John C. Hart et al. in 1986 [S1]. The method was developed to provide a competitive alternative to standard ray tracing for implicit surfaces, particularly excelling at rendering sophisticated geometric modeling operations [S1]. Early implementations focused on basic sphere tracing, with subsequent enhancements like convexity and triangle inequality optimizations improving performance for complex scenes [S1]. The technique has since become a foundational method in procedural geometry rendering and is widely used in real-time graphics, particularly in shader-based rendering and implicit surface visualization [S2, unofficial].
 
